{"parentUuid":"9c630fa3-4cf9-4231-9c45-e882de443827","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a944a82f6940ae23a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01Sg5jXZq1osE9AezB1JuF8T","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GBgnQMwyvDq32JUPMMKSGW","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"WebAssembly SIMD threads performance native benchmark 2025"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":20537,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":317,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":20537},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":6,"output_tokens":317,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":20537,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":20537,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaoxjfmQS3Hgytv4VsBTd","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"29eb3824-05a8-478c-ad17-39cf0535cdbc","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:01:26.102Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"cdb93741-46a9-4d3c-8f2a-9d8d5973b95c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"5ea2c3ce-bea8-4ce3-8195-f1f0f2991204","agentId":"a944a82f6940ae23a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tk7hb3SMznPNGkF28wvZY6","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CaDiCaL Kissat SAT solver WebAssembly port 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - arminbiere/kissat · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/arminbiere/kissat\"},{\"title\":\"FreshPorts -- math/kissat: Kissat SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://www.freshports.org/math/kissat/\"},{\"title\":\"CaDiCaL, Gimsatul, IsaSAT and Kissat Entering the SAT Competition 2025\",\"url\":\"https://cca.informatik.uni-freiburg.de/papers/BiereFallerFleuryFroleyksPollitt-SAT-Competition-2025-solvers.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"kissat: SAT solver - Packages and Features\",\"url\":\"https://doc.sagemath.org/html/en/reference/spkg/kissat.html\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - texmex76/kissat-cr\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/texmex76/kissat-cr\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - meelgroup/kissat-ml\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/meelgroup/kissat-ml\"},{\"title\":\"The Kissat SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://build.opensuse.org/package/show/science/kissat\"},{\"title\":\"Bug#1099368: kissat: transition from p7zip to 7zip\",\"url\":\"https://www.mail-archive.com/debian-science-maintainers@alioth-lists.debian.net/msg65126.html\"},{\"title\":\"Installation — cvc5 documentation - GitHub Pages\",\"url\":\"https://cvc5.github.io/docs/cvc5-1.0.8/installation/installation.html\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - jix/kissat_extras: A fork of the Kissat SAT solver with additional features. Supports incremental solving.\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/jix/kissat_extras\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about the CaDiCaL and Kissat SAT solvers and their 2025 competition entries, though the results don't contain specific details about a WebAssembly port in 2025.\n\nHere's what I found:\n\nKissat is a \"keep it simple and clean bare metal SAT solver\" written in C, which is a port of CaDiCaL back to C with improved data structures, better scheduling of inprocessing and optimized algorithms and implementation.\n\nRegarding 2025, a new submitted version of CaDiCaL is available on the development branch on Github, with its code base increased roughly from 46 KLOC to 64 KLOC of C++, needed to support techniques ported from Kissat. This appears to be related to the SAT Competition 2025 submissions.\n\nHowever, the search results do not contain specific information about a WebAssembly port of either Kissat or CaDiCaL announced in 2025. To get more detailed information about WebAssembly implementations, you might want to check the official GitHub repositories or contact the solver developers directly.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"830046da-5c9b-4b55-a111-12508819d4e5","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:01:30.497Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"cdb93741-46a9-4d3c-8f2a-9d8d5973b95c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"6deca025-01ee-4867-a044-aaadb67f905b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"5ea2c3ce-bea8-4ce3-8195-f1f0f2991204","agentId":"a944a82f6940ae23a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HV7oTJv9XZMYKYEx5kxBMz","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"OR-Tools WebAssembly WASM build 2025 2026 emscripten\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"WebAssembly Ecosystem 2026: Essential Tools, Frameworks & Runtimes\",\"url\":\"https://reintech.io/blog/webassembly-ecosystem-2026-tools-frameworks-runtimes\"},{\"title\":\"Building to WebAssembly — Emscripten 5.0.8-git (dev) documentation\",\"url\":\"https://emscripten.org/docs/compiling/WebAssembly.html\"},{\"title\":\"WebAssembly in 2026: How WASM is Democratizing Full-Stack Development | byteiota\",\"url\":\"https://byteiota.com/webassembly-in-2026-how-wasm-is-democratizing-full-stack-development/\"},{\"title\":\"WebAssembly 2026: Server-Side Runtimes, WASI, and the Universal Binary Revolution Beyond Browsers | Programming Helper Tech\",\"url\":\"https://www.programming-helper.com/tech/webassembly-2026-server-side-runtime-wasi-universal-binary\"},{\"title\":\"WebAssembly for Web Developers: Getting Started with Wasm in 2026\",\"url\":\"https://daily.dev/blog/webassembly-web-developers-getting-started-wasm\"},{\"title\":\"WebAssembly - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WebAssembly\"},{\"title\":\"The State of WebAssembly – 2025 and 2026\",\"url\":\"https://platform.uno/blog/the-state-of-webassembly-2025-2026/\"},{\"title\":\"WebAssembly 2025: Native Performance and Edge Computing Revolutionize the Web\",\"url\":\"https://blog.madrigan.com/en/blog/202603051459/\"},{\"title\":\"Cross-building with Emscripten - WebAssembly and asm.js — conan 2.27.1 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://docs.conan.io/2/examples/cross_build/emscripten.html\"},{\"title\":\"WebAssembly (WASM): The Future of High-Performance Web Applications - Amplifi Labs\",\"url\":\"https://www.amplifilabs.com/post/webassembly-wasm-the-future-of-high-performance-web-applications\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding OR-Tools WebAssembly WASM builds with Emscripten:\n\n## WebAssembly Ecosystem Status (2025-2026)\n\nFor existing C or C++ codebases like OR-Tools, Emscripten remains the most mature option, with version 3.x having significantly improved performance and reduced output size compared to earlier versions.\n\nEmscripten doesn't just compile code—it provides a complete environment that emulates POSIX APIs, making it possible to port complex applications with minimal changes.\n\n## Emscripten Compilation Process\n\nWebAssembly is emitted by default, without the need for any special flags. Emscripten emits WebAssembly using the upstream LLVM Wasm backend, since version 1.39.0 (October 2019).\n\n## Current Emscripten Capabilities\n\nEmscripten compiles C and C++ to Wasm using Clang as a frontend, Binaryen as an optimizer, and can also target any LLVM-supported language.\n\n## Performance Considerations\n\nThe trade-off is larger bundle sizes compared to Rust-based solutions.\n\nThe search results indicate that while Emscripten is well-suited for compiling C++ projects like OR-Tools to WebAssembly, specific information about OR-Tools WASM builds was not found in these results. You may want to check the official OR-Tools repository or documentation for their specific WASM build instructions and status.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"433811ba-6236-4236-8682-c808727ac90e","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:01:30.770Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6deca025-01ee-4867-a044-aaadb67f905b","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"9c630fa3-4cf9-4231-9c45-e882de443827","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"5ea2c3ce-bea8-4ce3-8195-f1f0f2991204","agentId":"a944a82f6940ae23a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QEXDVNFYHQ8u4GQ5tw1w1e","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"WASI 0.3 long running compute 2026 threads\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Hypercharge Through Components: Why WASI 0.3 and Composable Concurrency Are a Game Changer | by Enrico Piovesan | WebAssembly — WASM Radar | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/wasm-radar/hypercharge-through-components-why-wasi-0-3-and-composable-concurrency-are-a-game-changer-0852e673830a\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - WebAssembly/wasi-threads · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/WebAssembly/wasi-threads\"},{\"title\":\"WebAssembly in 2026: Where It Has Landed, What WASI 0.2 Changes, and Why Java and Kotlin Developers Should Pay Attention Now - Java Code Geeks\",\"url\":\"https://www.javacodegeeks.com/2026/04/webassembly-in-2026-where-it-has-landed-what-wasi-0-2-changes-and-why-java-and-kotlin-developers-should-pay-attention-now.html\"},{\"title\":\"Wasm Components & WASI Preview 3 at the Edge [2026]\",\"url\":\"https://techbytes.app/posts/wasm-components-wasi-preview-3-edge-optimization-2026/\"},{\"title\":\"Bytecode Alliance — Announcing wasi-threads\",\"url\":\"https://bytecodealliance.org/articles/wasi-threads\"},{\"title\":\"WASI 0.3 Native Async: WebAssembly Gets Concurrent I/O | byteiota\",\"url\":\"https://byteiota.com/wasi-0-3-native-async-webassembly-gets-concurrent-i-o/\"},{\"title\":\"WASI Preview 2 vs WASIX (2026): The WebAssembly System Interface Battle | wasmRuntime.com\",\"url\":\"https://wasmruntime.com/en/blog/wasi-preview2-vs-wasix-2026\"},{\"title\":\"WebAssembly: The High-Performance Web in 2026\",\"url\":\"https://blog.weskill.org/2026/04/webassembly-high-performance-web-in-2026.html\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction to WAMR WASI threads - WAMR\",\"url\":\"https://bytecodealliance.github.io/wamr.dev/blog/introduction-to-wamr-wasi-threads/\"},{\"title\":\"4 big changes WebAssembly developers need to know about | InfoWorld\",\"url\":\"https://www.infoworld.com/article/3966149/4-big-changes-webassembly-developers-need-to-know-about.html\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about WASI 0.3, long-running compute, threads, and 2026:\n\n## WASI 0.3 Overview\n\nWASI 0.3 released in February 2026 with native async/await support built directly into the WebAssembly Component Model. WASI 0.3 enhances the secure composition introduced in WASI 0.2 with native async support.\n\n## Long-Running Compute Support\n\nEvery component can manage I/O or long-running tasks without blocking others, and do so in a standardized, declarative way. WASI 0.3 defines new core types like stream<T> and future<T>, which makes async I/O first-class citizens in the component model.\n\n## Threading Status\n\nThreading remains a notable gap for WASI 0.3. Preview 3 is aimed primarily at async and thread support, with the subsequent WASI Preview 3 milestone primarily about adding async and thread support. However, adding multithreaded execution to WebAssembly isn't on the 0.3 roadmap, but there is an extant, approved proposal for doing it. The right 2026 posture for developers is to defer production WASI server migration until threading support lands.\n\n## Production Timeline\n\nWASI 1.0 production-stable is targeted for late 2026 or early 2027 according to the Bytecode Alliance roadmap.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"4261db51-4dc7-44bf-93e2-676abe5be06d","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:01:33.925Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9c630fa3-4cf9-4231-9c45-e882de443827","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"29eb3824-05a8-478c-ad17-39cf0535cdbc","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"5ea2c3ce-bea8-4ce3-8195-f1f0f2991204","agentId":"a944a82f6940ae23a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GBgnQMwyvDq32JUPMMKSGW","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"WebAssembly SIMD threads performance native benchmark 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Rust + WebAssembly 2025: Why WasmGC and SIMD Change Everything - DEV Community\",\"url\":\"https://dev.to/dataformathub/rust-webassembly-2025-why-wasmgc-and-simd-change-everything-3ldh\"},{\"title\":\"Boosting WebAssembly Performance with SIMD and Multi-Threading - InfoQ\",\"url\":\"https://www.infoq.com/articles/webassembly-simd-multithreading-performance-gains/\"},{\"title\":\"Rust + WebAssembly 2025: Why WasmGC and SIMD Change Everything | DataFormatHub\",\"url\":\"https://www.dataformathub.com/blog/rust-webassembly-2025-why-wasmgc-and-simd-change-everything-wrp\"},{\"title\":\"Rust WebAssembly Performance: 8-10x Faster (2025 Benchmarks) | byteiota\",\"url\":\"https://byteiota.com/rust-webassembly-performance-8-10x-faster-2025-benchmarks/\"},{\"title\":\"WebAssembly and SIMD: A match made in the browser | by Robert Aboukhalil | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://robaboukhalil.medium.com/webassembly-and-simd-7a7daa4f2ecd\"},{\"title\":\"8 WebAssembly SIMD Patterns for Data Crunching | by Nexumo | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@Nexumo_/8-webassembly-simd-patterns-for-data-crunching-8333c6c088b4\"},{\"title\":\"Using SIMD with WebAssembly — Emscripten 5.0.8-git (dev) documentation\",\"url\":\"https://emscripten.org/docs/porting/simd.html\"},{\"title\":\"[1901.09056] Not So Fast: Analyzing the Performance of WebAssembly vs. Native Code\",\"url\":\"https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1901.09056\"},{\"title\":\"Running High-Performance Code with WASM\",\"url\":\"https://blog.openreplay.com/running-high-performance-code-wasm/\"},{\"title\":\"Benchmarking WebAssembly for Embedded Systems | ACM Transactions on Architecture and Code Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3736169\"}]\n\nHere are the search results for WebAssembly SIMD threads performance native benchmark 2025:\n\n## Performance Gains\n\nBenchmarks from late 2025 show that WASM with SIMD can achieve 10-15x speedups over pure JavaScript for highly parallelizable workloads. More specifically, array operations drop from 1.4ms to 0.231ms—a 6x improvement—when SIMD vectorization kicks in.\n\n## Browser Support\n\nThe Single Instruction, Multiple Data (SIMD) proposal for WebAssembly has seen fantastic progress, with fixed-width 128-bit SIMD operations now widely supported across all major browsers, including Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge, Opera, and Samsung Internet, as of late 2024 and early 2025.\n\n## Threads and Threading Support\n\nAs of late 2025, WebAssembly 3.0 ships with WASM GC and threads, Memory64 support, SIMD, and proper exception handling in all major browsers. Threading support via SharedArrayBuffer and atomics enables true parallel computation. Combined with SIMD (Single Instruction, Multiple Data) operations, you can now run workloads that previously required native applications—video encoding, machine learning inference, and real-time audio processing.\n\n## Native Performance Comparison\n\nAll major browsers now boast highly optimized WASM support, with Chrome and Firefox consistently showing 95%+ of native performance for CPU-intensive tasks. WebAssembly with Rust delivers real performance gains—8-10x faster for compute-heavy browser tasks—backed by 2025 benchmarks and production deployments at Figma, Google, and Adobe.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"d8ddcfa3-2ffe-4385-8df2-928ab69baa27","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:01:40.596Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"29eb3824-05a8-478c-ad17-39cf0535cdbc","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstr