{"parentUuid":"29cd2627-f54c-4a89-a789-ea993206ad0e","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a944a82f6940ae23a","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01VhguXupPSpKvRpFTTQPKDQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TVuPuGVfC1HcN92dG7c46U","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"z3 wasm browser solver 2025 build"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5463,"cache_read_input_tokens":20537,"output_tokens":286,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5463},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":286,"cache_read_input_tokens":20537,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5463,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5463,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaoxmN27sMPTSRBZ5tY7q","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"c1d2f3bb-c46f-455f-93de-57c6be45227e","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:01:46.570Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"20619145-29bf-49d9-bbc2-1722e16dc5aa","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"5ea2c3ce-bea8-4ce3-8195-f1f0f2991204","agentId":"a944a82f6940ae23a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01456UwvgeXKu8rh9MLoxHP3","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"OR-Tools CP-SAT compile WebAssembly issue github\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CP-SAT crashes with address sanitizer · Issue #4269 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/4269\"},{\"title\":\"It is possible to compile Or Tools for webassembly? · Issue #4443 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/4443\"},{\"title\":\"[Help] Compiling OR-Tools to WebAssembly with Emscripten\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/ESK9oknRrK8\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT solver not finding solution · Issue #3643 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3643\"},{\"title\":\"Can't build ortools using cmake · Issue #4692 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/4692\"},{\"title\":\"Unable to import cp_model · Issue #3346 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3346\"},{\"title\":\"CP-Sat: Python 3.7.2, ortools 7.0 wrong optimization result · Issue #1162 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/1162\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT, UNKNOWN status with complete solution hint · Issue #3750 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3750\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"Multi-threaded CP-SAT solver is crashed · Issue #2001 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/2001\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about OR-Tools CP-SAT compilation for WebAssembly:\n\n## Key Findings\n\nThere is a GitHub issue (#4443) asking if it's possible to compile OR-Tools for WebAssembly to run constraint problems from the browser using either the C++ or Python interface.\n\nGoogle has been testing (still experimental) Emscripten builds with a GitHub workflow and Docker setup for WebAssembly compilation. When building with Emscripten, most third-party solvers are disabled so only CP-SAT and GLOP are available, and Emscripten is currently a low priority with no human resources allocated to it.\n\nThere was some experimental work on WebAssembly compilation almost five years ago, with attempts to compile to WASM, though this work has since been deprioritized.\n\nThe search results indicate that while WebAssembly compilation of OR-Tools has been attempted experimentally, it's not officially supported or actively maintained by Google at this time.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"363f8ca2-500c-46b5-9de2-3e9f08bf240d","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:01:50.437Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"20619145-29bf-49d9-bbc2-1722e16dc5aa","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"f99fb82e-2408-4492-ab36-0f3cd943ad76","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"5ea2c3ce-bea8-4ce3-8195-f1f0f2991204","agentId":"a944a82f6940ae23a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DGsSo3kxjq1GdqXow34Vix","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"wasmer wasmtime wasmedge runtime comparison 2026 production\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"WebAssembly Runtime Benchmarks 2026 | Wasmtime vs Wasmer vs WasmEdge | wasmRuntime.com\",\"url\":\"https://wasmruntime.com/en/benchmarks\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - wasmruntime-io/wasm-runtime-comparison: The Ultimate WebAssembly Runtime Comparison (2026). Benchmarks & Feature Matrix for Wasmtime, Wasmer, WasmEdge, Wazero, and WAMR. Best Wasm runtimes for Go, Python, and Rust. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/wasmruntime-io/wasm-runtime-comparison\"},{\"title\":\"Wasmtime vs Wasmer vs WasmEdge: WASM Runtime Comparison 2026 | Reintech media\",\"url\":\"https://reintech.io/blog/wasmtime-vs-wasmer-vs-wasmedge-wasm-runtime-comparison-2026\"},{\"title\":\"Comparing WebAssembly Runtimes: Wasmer vs. Wasmtime vs. Wasmedge — Unveiling the Power of Wasm | by Ashish Singh | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@siashish/comparing-webassembly-runtimes-wasmer-vs-wasmtime-vs-wasmedge-unveiling-the-power-of-wasm-ff1ecf2e64cd\"},{\"title\":\"Choosing a WebAssembly Run-Time\",\"url\":\"https://blog.colinbreck.com/choosing-a-webassembly-run-time/\"},{\"title\":\"Wasmer vs Wasmtime\",\"url\":\"https://wasmer.io/wasmer-vs-wasmtime\"},{\"title\":\"Performance of WebAssembly runtimes in 2023 - Frank DENIS random thoughts.\",\"url\":\"https://00f.net/2023/01/04/webassembly-benchmark-2023/\"},{\"title\":\"WebAssembly 2026: Server-Side Runtimes, WASI, and the Universal Binary Revolution Beyond Browsers | Programming Helper Tech\",\"url\":\"https://www.programming-helper.com/tech/webassembly-2026-server-side-runtime-wasi-universal-binary\"},{\"title\":\"Research on WebAssembly Runtimes: A Survey\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2404.12621v1\"},{\"title\":\"How does this compare to wasmtime, wasmer, etc? Why would I choose one over the ... | Hacker News\",\"url\":\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=33798129\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's a comprehensive comparison of Wasmer, Wasmtime, and WasmEdge runtimes in 2026 for production use:\n\n## Performance Comparison\n\nAs of January 2026, Wasm3 is fastest for cold start (interpreter), while among JIT/AOT compilers Wasmtime leads, followed by Wasmer; WasmEdge can close the gap with AOT optimization. More specifically, on edge devices with 1GB RAM, WasmEdge has an 8MB memory footprint and 1.5ms cold start time, Wasmtime has a 15MB footprint and 3ms cold start, while Wasmer has 12MB footprint and 2ms cold start.\n\n## Key Characteristics\n\n**Wasmtime:** The gold standard for WASI Preview 2 and Component Model support, with Wasmtime leading in implementation. Wasmtime, developed by the Bytecode Alliance, implements WASI and serves as the foundation for numerous production deployments.\n\n**Wasmer:** Wasmer has the easiest pip install and aims for fast startup times and low runtime overhead through optimizations like ahead-of-time (AOT) and just-in-time (JIT) compilation.\n\n**WasmEdge:** WasmEdge supports native AI hardware acceleration for lower inference latency. WasmEdge is optimized for edge computing, supporting TensorFlow Lite for AI inference at the edge, Kubernetes integration, and plugin systems.\n\n## Future Trends\n\nThe WASM Component Model is gaining traction, with Wasmtime leading in implementation and Wasmer and WasmEdge catching up throughout 2026. All three runtimes are implementing WASI Preview 2, which brings async I/O and improved resource management and will narrow performance gaps for I/O-bound workloads.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"0bdfd68a-1748-43d5-b35c-98363615460c","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:01:53.412Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f99fb82e-2408-4492-ab36-0f3cd943ad76","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"29cd2627-f54c-4a89-a789-ea993206ad0e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"5ea2c3ce-bea8-4ce3-8195-f1f0f2991204","agentId":"a944a82f6940ae23a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014tKJT9jwehgtyeTsna2X5E","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"SAT solver browser demo WebAssembly minisat z3 wasm\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - cpitclaudel/z3.wasm: WASM builds of the Z3 SMT solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/cpitclaudel/z3.wasm\"},{\"title\":\"MiniSat.js\",\"url\":\"https://jgalenson.github.io/research.js/demos/minisat.html\"},{\"title\":\"Z3.wasm demo\",\"url\":\"https://people.csail.mit.edu/cpitcla/z3.wasm/z3.html\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - glnc/minisat-wasm: Compile MiniSat to WebAssembly using emscripten\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/glnc/minisat-wasm\"},{\"title\":\"MiniSat in your browser | Wonderings of a SAT geek\",\"url\":\"https://www.msoos.org/2013/09/minisat-in-your-browser/\"},{\"title\":\"Z3 Playground\",\"url\":\"https://jfmc.github.io/z3-play/\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - ViRb3/z3-wasm: Run Z3 entirely in your browser using WebAssembly\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/ViRb3/z3-wasm\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - Z3Prover/z3: The Z3 Theorem Prover · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Z3Prover/z3\"},{\"title\":\"z3-solver - npm\",\"url\":\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/z3-solver\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - styczynski/go-sat-solver: SAT solver written in Go (based on Minisat)\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/styczynski/go-sat-solver\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here are several SAT solver and SMT solver browser demos available with WebAssembly:\n\n## Z3 WASM Demos\n\nA Z3 demo is available at https://people.csail.mit.edu/cpitcla/z3.wasm/z3.html. This repository contains a build script to compile Z3 to WebAssembly using emscripten, and loading Z3 is fairly slow (~15 seconds on Chrome, though less than 1 second on Firefox), but verification typically is within a factor 2 to 5 of native performance.\n\nThere's also a simple Z3 playground based on the Monaco Editor, with acknowledgments to the original rise4fun Z3 reconstruction and z3-wasm available at https://jfmc.github.io/z3-play/.\n\nAdditionally, a WebAssembly build with associated TypeScript typings is published on npm as z3-solver.\n\n## MiniSat WASM Demos\n\nInstructions on how to build MiniSat with Emscripten are available on GitHub, and you can try the demo at https://jgalenson.github.io/research.js/demos/minisat.html.\n\nMiniSat can be compiled to WebAssembly using emscripten, with a fork of MiniSat v2.2.0 available. A demo web app can be built and run on http://localhost:8080.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"3a7f929d-87d4-487f-b6fe-3ad26f5410a6","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:01:53.565Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"29cd2627-f54c-4a89-a789-ea993206ad0e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"c1d2f3bb-c46f-455f-93de-57c6be45227e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"5ea2c3ce-bea8-4ce3-8195-f1f0f2991204","agentId":"a944a82f6940ae23a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TVuPuGVfC1HcN92dG7c46U","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"z3 wasm browser solver 2025 build\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - cpitclaudel/z3.wasm: WASM builds of the Z3 SMT solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/cpitclaudel/z3.wasm\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - bramvdbogaerde/z3-wasm: Scripts and Javascript Glue code to use Z3 in the browser using WASM\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/bramvdbogaerde/z3-wasm\"},{\"title\":\"z3-solver - npm\",\"url\":\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/z3-solver\"},{\"title\":\"Replicating Rise4Fun Z3 with z3-wasm | Hey There Buddo!\",\"url\":\"https://www.philipzucker.com/replacing-rise4fun/\"},{\"title\":\"WASM Builds of the Z3 SMT Solver | Hacker News\",\"url\":\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=20489909\"},{\"title\":\"Releases · cpitclaudel/z3.wasm\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/cpitclaudel/z3.wasm/releases\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - ViRb3/z3-wasm: Run Z3 entirely in your browser using WebAssembly\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/ViRb3/z3-wasm\"},{\"title\":\"z3 browser init can't work as directed · Issue #7556 · Z3Prover/z3\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Z3Prover/z3/issues/7556\"},{\"title\":\"z3.wasm/z3.sh at master · cpitclaudel/z3.wasm\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/cpitclaudel/z3.wasm/blob/master/z3.sh\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - bakkot/z3-js-bindings: WIP bindings for the Z3 solver in JS\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/bakkot/z3-js-bindings\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about Z3 WASM browser solver builds:\n\n## Main Projects and Resources\n\nPre-build archives are available at https://github.com/cpitclaudel/z3.wasm/releases, with a demo at https://people.csail.mit.edu/cpitcla/z3.wasm/z3.html.\n\nThe z3-wasm repository contains scripts that make interaction with the Z3 solver from a browser using WASM easier, with build.sh containing commands to fetch and compile Z3 using emscripten.\n\nThe z3-solver project provides TypeScript bindings for the Z3 theorem prover and is available on npm as z3-solver, with the latest version being 4.16.0.\n\n## Building Z3 WASM\n\nThe first build can take up to two hours, and the output (z3w.js, z3w.wasm, z3smt2w.js, and z3smt2w.wasm) is written to build/z3/.\n\nThe build script produces two artifacts: a copy of the full Z3 command line application (z3w.js, z3w.wasm), and a Javascript version of a small SMT2 REPL-style API.\n\n## Browser Compatibility\n\nThe z3-solver package requires threads, which means you'll need an environment that supports SharedArrayBuffer, and in browsers, you'll need to serve your page with special headers.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"1d498ae8-c40a-4609-977e-c7b48e7961a2","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:01:54.297Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c1d2f3bb-c46f-455f-93de-57c6be45227e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
